title HUNK6 — the marketplace sprite sheet is a content-addressed artifact
prompt Continue the loop: next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, self-approve design rounds, send each finished increment as its own signed round, STOP at custody gates
think HUNK4 (the index) and HUNK5 (the seen page) both trust the sheet in hand. The next agent-doable crux the design round named is HUNK6: give the sheet an identity, so a marketplace can only be served from the exact bytes its name pins. It composes HUNK1's image_artifact (store_image/fetch_image) with HUNK4's SpriteIndex.
think The crux to prove: a tampered sheet refuses DigestMismatch BEFORE any SpriteIndex is built or product rendered — content-addressed storage is the marketplace's integrity guarantee for free, the pattern HUNK1 gave a single image now carried to the whole sheet a catalog windows into.
think Home in pond/apps/ where image_artifact + tablecloth + beading live; symlinked sprite.rye + photos.rye in (qoi + tally_copy already there). open_sheet is HUNK1's verified fetch; store_sheet is HUNK1's store_image, both named for the market.
obs Checked the real HUNK1 API before building — BeadStore/Catalog are .empty() not .init()/{}, the report fields are beads_deposited/bead_count/beads_deduped, tamper is a direct store_t.beads[0].bytes[0] +%= 1, stored length via tablecloth.artifact_len. GREEN on the first build and run after matching that API.
obs Proven on metal: sheet stored (54 bytes over 1 bead), a market opened over the verified sheet renders two products from their own windows byte-for-byte, an unknown market refuses UnknownArtifact, and a single flipped stored byte makes open_sheet refuse DigestMismatch before any product renders. tame_style_check exit 0, width exit 0; HUNK1 image_artifact re-run GREEN beneath.
obs Additive and reuse-only — no change to image_artifact.rye, sprite.rye, tablecloth.rye, or beading. No breach, so no cairn. No network, key, or funds — hosting a real sheet stays custody gates #2/#3.
